      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1205,
      "output": "**Water** is a chemical substance that is essential for all known forms of life. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless liquid that is the main component of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, from a chemical and physical perspective:\n\n### 1. Chemical Composition\nAt its most basic level, water is a molecule. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of:\n*   **Two hydrogen atoms**\n*   **One oxygen atom**\n\nThese atoms are chemically bonded together in a bent shape, which gives water its unique properties.\n\n### 2. States of Matter\nWater is unique because it can exist naturally in all three states of matter under standard Earth conditions:\n*   **Solid:** When frozen, it is called **ice**.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we see most often (rain, oceans, drinking water).\n*   **Gas:** When heated, it turns into **water vapor** or steam.\n\n### 3. Key Physical Properties\nWater has several properties that make it distinct from other liquids:\n*   **Polarity:** Because of how the atoms are arranged, water is a \"polar molecule.\" This means one end of the molecule has a slight positive charge and the other has a slight negative charge. This allows water molecules to stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion).\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Water is excellent at dissolving other substances. This is why it is often called the \"universal solvent.\" It helps transport nutrients and waste inside living things.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together very strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on water.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the temperature of the Earth and keeps the human body stable.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the foundation of life. It makes up about **60% of the human body** and is involved in almost every biological process, including:\n*   Transporting nutrients and oxygen to cells.\n*   Removing waste (urine, sweat).\n*   Regulating body temperature through sweating and panting.\n*   Lubricating joints and protecting sensitive tissues (like the eyes and spinal cord)."
